\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The Hungarian HexaHedr
on is an interesting and difficult puzzle.  It is a cube of three layers in each direction.  Each layer may be moved independently without disturbing the others.  A movement consists of a 90 or 180 degree turn about the center of the layer.  Each face of 
t
he Cube is labeled with a color.  Each face is cut into nine "facelets" so there are nine labels in each of the six colors.  Moving the layers scrambles the labels, so the puzzle is to get all the same colored labels back together on the same faces, witho
u
t peeling off the labels or taking the Cube apart.  Neither peeling nor dismantling is possible with this program, but the latter was frequently necessary with real Cubes marketed by Ideal Toy Corp. in the early 1980's. This program adds to the labels the
 color of adjacent facelets, making it easier to see what is around a corner and creating a distinctive overall appearance.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cube Anatomy (Cube Basics)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Cube Anatomy
\par }{\f1 
\par If you did dismantle a real Cube marketed by Ideal Toy Corp. in the early 1980's, the six center facelets would stay stuck together by springs to a plastic center spindle, but 20 other parts would fall free, 12 edge \ldblquote cubies\rdblquote 
 and 8 corner "cubies".  The corner cubies have three labels each and form the corners of the original Cube.  The edge cubies have two labels each a
nd fill the space between the corner cubies and the center cubies.  Just as cubies form the Cube in space, facelets form each face in the area visible on the outside of the Cube.  Each facelet is associated with one label.  A face, technically, is only on
e
 surface of the Cube but a layer is one-third the volume of the whole Cube and consists of one center cubie, four edge cubies, and four corner cubies.  When the Cube is scrambled, the whole layer is moved, not just the face, but this is usually called a "
face move" anyway.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Moves (Cube Basics)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Moves}{\f1 
\par 
\par A Cube can be completely scrambled and solved using face moves.  There are six faces and three moves per face.  The three moves are a quarter turn of 90 degrees clockwise, a half turn that turns the layer upside down, and a thre
e quarter turn of 270 degrees clockwise, but it is easily to turn one quarter counterclockwise.  Besides these 18 face moves, there are four other commonly used types of moves.  A Cube Move, turns the entire Cube.  A Slice Move, turns the two outer layers
 without moving the middle layer.  A Middle Move turns the middle layer without moving the two outer layers.  And an Antislice Move turns the two outer layers in opposite directions.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Standard Color Pattern (Cube Basics)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Standard Color Pattern}{\f1 
\par 
\par If only Face, Slice and Antislice Moves are use
d, the center facelets never move so it is easy to use the colors of the centers as a frame of reference and say the Cube is solved when the other facelets on a face match the center.  On the other hand, since moving the whole Cube is called a "move", the
n
 even if all the colors on the faces match but the Cube is upside down, it is not considered solved until it is turned right side up.  This program always solves a Cube by not only matching the colors, but also rotating the whole Cube so the colors lie in
 a standard pattern.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Orientation (Cube Basics)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Orientation}{\f1 
\par 
\par In this program when the Cube is not in motion, it is always shown in the same orientation.  A move turns one or more layers about the center of a layer.  Even the Cube Move works this way.  Cube, Slice, Middle, and Anti
slice Moves are similar to the Face Move because a face is chosen and a quarter or half turn is carried out, moving one, two or even all three layers.
\par 
\par See also }{\f1\uldb Cube History}{\v\f1\uldb cube_history}{\f1 
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ the_main_cube}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ The Main Cube}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super +
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:005}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Main Cube}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 The Main Cube}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Two Cubes (The Main Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Two Cubes}{\f1 
\par 
\par Unfortunately, one picture of a 
Cube only shows you half of it since the rest is in back.  This program shows two views of the Cube at all times so you can visualize what is behind the Cube.  The second view is what you would see if you could see through the Cube.  Another way to think 
about it is to suppose you were holding a Cube and standing in front of a mirror.  You would see two Cubes, one in the mirror and the real one.
\par 
\par When the mouse cursor is moved over the Cube, a hand cursor is shown.  This looks like a right hand, but if you 
were standing in front of a mirror holding the front of the Cube with your right hand and the back of the Cube with your left hand, you would see the reflection of your left hand in the mirror and it would look like your right.
\par 
\par You can suppose you are eit
her looking through the Cube or seeing the reflection in a mirror.  Either way, the second view of the Cube is provided to help you visualize the whole Cube without having to turn it.  But you can also rotate the whole Cube to see what is behind.  
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Standard Color Pattern (The Main Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Standard Color Pattern}{\f1 
\par 
\par When the Cube is not in motion, it is always shown in the same orientation, looking corner-on.  There is also a standard set of colors and names for the faces.  When Cubes were manufactured by Ideal Toy Corp. in the early 1980's, a stan
dard set and pattern of colors was usually followed, but not always.  This program follows the standard color pattern and if you have a Cube with a different pattern, you will have to transpose.  It would probably be more confusing than useful for this pr
ogram to allow the colors to be redefined.  It is also useful, because the whole Cube can be rotated, to define the faces by directions rather than colors.  A standard set of face direction names has also been established.
\par 
\par In this program, the Front, Up, and Right faces are shown in the left image of the Cube.  The Left, Down, and Back faces are shown in the right image.  The colors are arranged as follows:
\par 
\par \{bml diagram.bmp\}
\par 
\par This program adds to the labels the color of adjacent facelets, making it easier to see what is around a corner and creating a distinctive overall appearance.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Notation (The Main Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Notation}{\f1 
\par 
\par A notation has also been established which uses the first letter of each face name as its symbol.  So Front is "F" for example.  A phonetic system was also developed
, but it was inconvenient that the first letter for Up is "U", a vowel.  The phonetic system uses "T" for Top.  All the other face names are standard.  This program uses the name Up and the letter "U" but "T" is recognized as a synonym.
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ moving_the_cube}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Moving the Cube}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super +
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:006}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Moving the Cube}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 Moving the Cube}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
The Cube can be rotated using the mouse or by selecting a Move command from the menu.  Move the mouse over the Cube while holding down the mouse buttons and the Ctrl key.  The movement of the mouse determines the direction and distance the Cube sho
uld move and the combination of buttons determines the layers that move.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Hand Cursor (Moving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Hand Cursor}{\f1 
\par 
\par When the mouse is positioned over any face of the Cube, the usual arrow cursor is replaced with a closed hand cursor.  The hand is shown at a different angle for each 
face.  When a mouse button is pressed, the hand opens to indicate it has gripped the face and is ready to move.  The face rotates about its center.  Dragging the mouse with a mouse button down moves the face.  When the button is released, the face snaps t
o the nearest quarter turn position, restoring the cube shape.  The Ctrl key on the keyboard also acts like a mouse button.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Drag and Drop (Moving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Drag and Drop}{\f1 
\par 
\par The face follows the angle of the cursor with the center of the face.  The cursor must be over the face when the bu
tton is pushed down, but the cursor may move off the face while the button is held, so long as the cursor is not moved out of the window.  If the cursor is moved out of the window, the button is considered released and the face snaps to the nearest quarte
r
 turn.  If the button is pushed when it is not over a face, it is ignored and must be pushed again when the cursor is over a face.  Since it is the angle of the cursor with the center of the face that is important, the distance from the center is not impo
r
tant, but if you put the cursor very near the center it will be hard to tell what the angle is and the face will appear to jump around uncontrollably.  The precise position of the cursor is the middle knuckle of the index finger.  For the best control, po
sition the cursor over a corner facelet and drag the mouse to another corner.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Mouse Buttons (Moving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Mouse Buttons}{\f1 
\par 
\par There are six different types of moves possible with the mouse:
\par 
