\f1 \row }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 Right button\cell Opposite Face Move\cell Far layer\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 \row }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 Left & Middle buttons\cell Slice Move\cell 
Near and far layers\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 \row }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 Middle button\cell Middle Move\cell Middle layer\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft7 
\clvertalt\cltxlrtb \cellx2340\clvertalt\cltxlrtb \cellx4410\clvertalt\cltxlrtb \cellx7470\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 Right & Middle buttons\cell Antislice Move\cell Near and far layers, far layer moves in the opposite direction\cell }\pard 
\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\f1 \row }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
\par If your mouse has only two buttons, you don\rquote t have a Middle button, but you can use the Ctrl key on the keyboard in its place.
\par 
\par If you're working the mouse left handed and have swapped the buttons, you will have to account for this, of course.  The Middle and Opposite Face Moves seem to work a little strangely because the face the hand is on does not move.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Move Commands (Moving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Move Commands}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can also move the Cube using "Move" commands on the menu.  Select "Move", the face and type of move you want, and how far to turn the Cube.  The standard direction 
for turns is always expressed in print as clockwise looking at that face.  So the menu lists \'bc, \'bd and \'be turns.  The \'be turn is actually carried out by turning \'bc
 counterclockwise.  However, since the second image of the Cube on the screen is a mirror image, 
moves on that Cube look like they're reversed again.  The system of always specifying turns in the clockwise direction looking at the face was developed to eliminate confusion for people working with Cubes in real life where it is easier to remember to us
e the same wrist motion when reaching behind the Cube.  It makes this program harder to use, but it is consistent with the idea that the second image of the Cube is seen through a mirror.
\par 
\par There are shortcuts using the Function key for Face Moves.  Front is F2, Up F3, Right F4, Left F5, Down F6, and Back F7.  By themselves, they do \'bc clockwise turns.  With the Ctrl key held down, they do \'bc
 counterclockwise turns.  There are similar shortcuts for Cube Moves, by holding down the Shift key.
\par }{\b\f1\fs24 \page }{\cs16\b\f1\fs24\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ editing_the_cube}}}{\b\f1\fs24  }{\cs16\b\f1\fs24\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{
 Editing the Cube}}}{\b\f1\fs24  }{\cs16\b\f1\fs24\super +{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:007}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Editing the Cube}}}{\b\f1\fs24  }{
\b\f1\fs24\cf2 Editing the Cube}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
If you have a real Cube marketed by Ideal Toy Corp. in the early 1980's that is scrambled and you want use this program to help solve it, you need "Edit Cube".  This command is the last item under "Edit" on the menu of the main window. When you sele
ct this command, a second window appears with a Cube like the original but at the bottom it says "Edit Cube" instead of the normal Generator box in the main window.  Also, the menus and mouse commands are different.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Changing Colors (Editing the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Changing Colors}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you move the mous
e over the colored part of a facelet and push the left button, a menu of colors appears.  Drag the mouse down the menu until you get to the color you want and release the button.  The facelet will change to that color.  To help clarify which menu item the
 mouse is over, the name of that color is printed over the item.  When you are finished, close the window by selecting "Close" from the menu.  You can't use any other window in this program until you are finished with this window.
\par 
\par Another way to change colors is to press the right button repeatedly over a facelet.  The color will change each time.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Standard Color Pattern (Editing the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Standard Color Pattern}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can rearrange the colors any way you want, but there is a standard color pattern that you can't change.  The program will leave you
 alone while you edit the Cube, but when you try to close the window, the program will complain if you have violated any rules about nonsense color arrangements, unsolvable arrangements or if you violate the standard color pattern.
\par 
\par The standard color pattern is described under }{\f1\uldb The Main Cube}{\v\f1 the_main_cube}{\f1 
.  If your Cube has a non-standard pattern, you'll have problems when you try to close the window.  To test if your Cube is non-standard, do this: if your Cube is scrambled, look only at the center facelets 
in the middle of each face.  Now look at the opposite faces.  You should have red opposite orange, yellow opposite white and blue opposite green.  If all that is OK, hold the Cube so that red is in front and yellow is to the right.  Now look at the Up (to
p
) face.  It should be blue.  If it is green, then you will have to transpose green and blue when representing your Cube into the program.  If you had a problem with the opposite faces not pairing off correctly or if you have a Cube with an entirely differ
ent color, you will have to transpose these somehow to match the standard pattern.  You should be able to turn the whole Cube to get at least a few of the colors in the standard pattern so you won't have to do so much transposing in your head.
\par 
\par If you follo
w the standard color pattern, you don't have to have the center facelet of the front red, the right yellow or the top blue when you enter the colors of your Cube into the program, but it must be possible for the program to rotate the whole Cube so that th
e standard pattern is restored, otherwise the program will refuse to update the main Cube with your changes when you close the edit window.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cube Dismantling (Editing the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Cube Dismantling}{\f1 
\par 
\par There are two other of problems you might have with a real Cube marketed by Ideal Toy Corp. in t
he early 1980's.  If someone has peeled off and rearranged the labels or if the Cube has been taken apart and reassembled, your Cube may be unsolvable.  If it is, this program will complain.  If you are sure you've enter the colors correctly into the prog
r
am, but it refuses to accept it, your Cube has been tampered with.  Take the Cube apart, reassemble it so that it is solved, and then scramble it and try again.  If the labels were peeled off, you won't be able to reassemble it in the solved position.  If
 it was dismantled and reassembled by someone else incorrectly, you will have corrected that problem.
\par 
\par To dismantle a Cube, rotate one face 45 degrees and pry out an edge cubie from the rotated face by pulling the rotated face away from the rest of the Cube
.  When there is enough clearance between the edge cubie in the rotated face and the edge cubie in the middle layer below it, the cubie in the rotated face can be moved out away from the center of the rotated face.  When you have pried this cubie loose, i
mmediately put it back and study what you have done.  The Cube is very easy to dismantle after you've removed one cubie, but it is a good idea to proceed slowly so you can see the steps you will need for reassembly.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Error Messages (Editing the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Error Messages}{\f1 
\par 
\par Here is a list of complaints the program can make.  Of course, the most likely problem is that you made a mistake entering the colors into the program, so check that first:
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Duplicate center facelet color.}{\f1 
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 Each of the six center facelets must be all six different colors.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Non-standard center facelet color pattern.}{\f1 
\par \tab This is the problem described earlier in this section.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Too many facelets of one color.}{\f1 
\par \tab There are 54 facelets total and six colors, so there must be nine facelets of each color.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Edge cubie colors do not match centers.}{\f1 
\par \tab The 12 edge cubies have two colors each.  There must be one edge cubie to match each adjacent pair of faces with colors to match the center facelets.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Corner cubie colors do not match centers.}{\f1 
\par \tab The 8 corners cubies have three colors each.  There must b
e one corner cubie to match each corner where three faces come together.  Even if the three colors match the centers of those faces, if two of the labels were peeled off and swapped the labels would not match the orientation of the faces, so check careful
ly.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Cube unsolvable: Flip parity.}{\f1 
\par \tab There is something wrong with your edge cubies probably because the Cube was dismantled.  Your edge cubie colors are OK, so it is possible to get them all back into their proper locations.  Once in their proper locations,
 they could be flipped in place the wrong way so the colors are reversed from their adjacent centers.  The total number of edge cubies flipped in place can be either even or odd, but it can never be changed from an odd number to an even number unless the 
Cube is taken apart.  If the number is odd, at least one edge will always be flipped in place and your Cube cannot be solved.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Cube unsolvable: Twist parity.}{\f1 
\par \tab There is something wrong with your corner cubies probably because the Cube was dismantled.  Your c
orner cubie colors are OK, so it is possible to get them all back into their proper locations.  Once in their proper locations, they could be twisted in place 120 degrees or 240 degrees so the colors do not match the centers of the adjacent faces.  The to
t
al amount of twist of all corners combined can be an exact multiple of 360 degrees or, if the Cube was taken apart, there can be an extra 120 or 240 degrees.  If the total is not a multiple of 360 degrees, at least one corner will always be twisted in pla
ce and your Cube cannot be solved.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Cube unsolvable: Swap parity.}{\f1 
\par \tab There is something wrong with your edge or corner cubies probably because the Cube was dismantled.  Your corner and edge cubie colors are OK and it is possible to get either all your corners
 or all your edges back to their proper locations, but it is not possible to do both.  Regardless of flipping edges or twisting corners in place, you can analyze restoring your Cube as swapping pairs of edges or corners.  The total number of pair swaps ca
n be either even or odd, but it can never be changed from an odd number to an even number unless the Cube is taken apart.  If the number is odd, at least one pair of cubies will always be swapped out of place and your Cube cannot be solved.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Menu Commands (Editing the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Menu Commands}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can also Edit the Cube by selecting commands from the menu, but this is awkward.  Select the face you want to edit from the menu: Front, Up, Right, Left, Down, or Back.  Under that menu is a sub-menu listing the cubies in that face in lower case lett
ers.  The lower case letters identify the cubie by the faces in which it lies.  The center cubie has one letter, edges have two, and corners have three.  After you select the cubie, the list of colors appears.  This list is different from the list you get
 
when you use the mouse.  This list of colors is just the names in black and white.  The whole mechanism is quite tedious but it's only there in case you have no mouse.  In the list of color names, one is "grayed out" and cannot be selected because the fac
elet is already that color.  That might give you a clue as to where you are in the menu.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Closing the Window (Editing the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Closing the Window}{\f1 
\par 
\par When you finish with the Edit Cube window if there are any problems the program will ask you if you want to throw away the bogus Cube and go back
 to the main Cube as you left it.  You can choose Cancel if you want to continue editing.  If the Cube is OK, it will ask you if you want to use the new Cube and "regenerate".  If you say no, your editing will be thrown away.  If you say Cancel you can co
ntinue editing.  If you say yes, the main Cube will be updated, the Edit Cube window will disappear and, indeed, it will "regenerate."  Regeneration is discussed in the section }{\f1\uldb Solving the Cube}{\v\f1\uldb solving_the_cube}{\f1 
 and the Generator, generally, is discussed in the section }{\f1\uldb The Generator}{\v\f1\uldb the_generator}{\f1 . If you haven't gotten to those sections yet, just say yes.
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ the_generator}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ The Generator}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super +
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:008}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Generator}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 The Generator}{\b\f1\fs24 
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 If you move the Cube with the mouse, or select "Move" commands from the menu, the Generator box at the bottom of the main window lists
 the moves in "cube notation".  You can type moves into the Generator box yourself using cube notation.  When you finish typing or editing the Generator, press the Enter key and the program will move the Cube to match the Generator.  
\par 
\par While you are workin
g in the Generator box, the program will leave you alone but if you try to do something else, the program will get upset because it likes to keep the main Cube up to date with the Generator.  If the Generator has been changed and you try to move the Cube 
w
ith the mouse, select a command from the menu, or press the Esc key, this program will ask you about the Generator.  You can choose to update the Cube with the Generator, throw away the changes you made to the Generator, or cancel the command.  You will a
lso be prevented from switching to another window within this program.
\par 
\par So when you are finished typing in the Generator box, press Enter to update the Cube before you try to do anything else.  You can press the Esc key instead if you want to throw away you
r changes, but the program will ask you if you're sure, because Windows programs have maternal instincts.
\par 
\par To update the main Cube quickly, press Tab and the program will skip the animation.  These commands are also listed under \ldblquote Edit\rdblquote  on the menu.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Generator Matches Main Cube (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Generator Matches Main Cube}{\f1 
\par 
\par The cube notation in the Generator box includes the moves put there automatically when you move the Cube using the mouse or selecting a "Move" command, but there's a lot more you can do and there are other ways that the Generator b
ox can be changed.  The sections }{\f1\uldb Editing the Cube}{\v\f1\uldb editing_the_cube}{\f1  and }{\f1\uldb Solving the Cube}{\v\f1\uldb solving_the_cube}{\f1 
 describe other things you can do with this program that effect the main Cube and change the Generator to reflect the new Cube.  No matter what you do, th
is program keeps track of a move sequence in the Generator box that matches the main Cube.  If you ever get disgusted and want the main Cube solved, just delete all the text in the Generator box and press Enter.  Neither the number of characters nor moves
 in the Generator box may exceed 8,000.  Of course characters and moves are different things, but the limits are the same and nothing good will happen if you exceed them.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Simple Generators (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Simple Generators}{\f1 
\par 
\par The code in the Generator box can get sophisticated because the 
Cube has a lot to do with "Group Theory" in mathematics.  The Generator can contain a label, moves, repeat numbers, conjugates, and commutators.  But usually a Generator is just a label and a sequence of ordinary moves.  The Generator is like a simple sta
t
ement in computer language but if any part of it is incorrect, this program just ignores that part and uses the rest.  The first part of the Generator is a label which can be anything you want, then a ":" (colon) and then the equation.  If there is anothe
r
 colon in your Generator, the second one is ignored because the label stops with the first colon and the program thinks the second colon is in the middle of the equation.  If your Generator has no colon, the program thinks you have no label and thinks the
 only thing in the Generator box is the equation.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Saving to Disk (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Saving to Disk}{\f1 
\par 
\par When you run this program for the first time, the label is "Scratch" and there is a colon and the equation is empty.  An empty equation is OK.  It means the Cube is in the solved position 
and no moves have been made yet.  When you Exit this program for the first time, it will ask you if you want your Cube saved.  If you say yes, the Generator will be saved in a file called SCRATCH.CUB in the directory with this program.  You can also save 
the Cube to disk any time it is out of date by selecting "Save" under "File" on the menu.  You can also press Ctrl+S.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Clipboard (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Clipboard}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can also mark text, Cut or Copy it to or Paste it from the Clipboard, or just Delete it.  To a limited extent you can undo changes, but this doesn't work very well because of interactions with other parts of the program.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cube Notation (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Cube Notation}{\f1 
\par 
\par The equation in the Generator Box is a sequence of moves and operations on moves.  A sequence is followed from left to right carrying out 
those moves on a Cube from its original, solved position.  If you did that yourself, you would get the main Cube.  The moves are represented by letters, so it looks like an algebra equation with everything multiplied together, but be careful, the rules of
 algebra don't apply.  If the equation is FR it means move the Front face, then move the Right face.  But FR isn't the same as RF because you aren't multiplying, you're following a sequence.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Moves (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Moves}{\f1 
\par 
\par The moves are described in the section }{\f1\uldb Moving the Cube}{\v\f1\uldb moving_the_cube}{\f1 
.  Every move has three parts: the face to move, the type of move and how far to turn.  In cube notation, the face is a letter F, B, R, L, U, or D.  You can also use T for Top in place of U for Up, if you prefer.  Immediately following the fac
e letter is a letter for the move type c, s, m, or a for Cube, Slice, Middle or Antislice.  If none of these four letters appear, it is a Face Move.  It is customary to capitalize the face letter and not the move type letter, but the program accepts upper
 
and lower case for the face or the move type.  Whether there is a move type letter or not, the next character can be a single quote ' or a number (or both) indicating how far to turn.  The number 2 indicates a half turn.  The single quote, called a prime,
 means a \'be clockwise or \'bc counterclockwise turn.  If there is no quote or number, it means a \'bc turn clockwise.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Repeat Numbers (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Repeat Numbers}{\f1 
\par 
\par The number is like an exponent and a prime is like a minus sign in the exponent.  So F1 is the same as F, F2 is like FF and F3 
is like FFF.  And F4 doesn't do anything at all because four quarter turns comes right back to the start.  F' is an exponent of minus one and is the same as F3.  You can use any number you want, but since you've already got F and F', the only number you n
eed is 2.  To make the equation easier to read, whenever you enter 2 like an exponent, it is printed as a superscript such as F\'b2 and can be read as "F squared."
\par 
\par Besides listing sequences of moves, you can specify that a sequence be carried out repeatedly b
y enclosing the sequence in parentheses and following it with a single quote, a number or both.  So, (FR)3 is the same as FRFRFR.  Using the single quote, or inverse, on a sequence of moves begins to get sophisticated, but it basically means to undo the s
e
quence.  Obviously, the way to undo F is to do F', but to undo a sequence you must also reverse the sequence.  The way to undo FR is to do R'F' but using this program you could write (FR)'.  Of course if you do any sequence and then undo it, the Cube is s
olved.  So the equation FURDLFUR(FURDLFUR)' is a solved Cube.  The equation (FR)3(FR)3' is also a solved Cube.  The equation (FR)105 is a solved Cube, but that's another story.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Conjugates and Commutators (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Conjugates and Commutators}{\f1 
\par 
\par The most sophisticated operations available in th
e Generator box are conjugates and commutators.  In this program a conjugate is represented with a star * and a commutator with a plus sign +.  For any equations X and Y, X * Y = X Y X' and X + Y = X Y X' Y'.  These operations are used in group theory, bu
t
 this notation is non-standard and will create confusing results if not used in the manner defined here.  These operations are left to right associative.  The repeat number and inverse operations have the highest precedence so RF' = R(F').  The sequence o
peration has higher precedence than * so RF * U = (RF) * U.  The lowest precedence is +, so R+F * U = R+(F * U).  It is an interesting property of group theory that X * (Y+Z) = X * Y+X * Z, but generally the rules of algebra do not apply.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Unrecognized Characters (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Unrecognized Characters}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can use spaces for legibility anywhere except between the face letter and the move type letter.  Any characters not recognized are ignored.  If the parentheses are not paired off, the program assumes there are enough left parentheses at the b
eginning of the equation and right parentheses at the end to balance them out.  Anywhere a move or sequence of moves is missing, an empty sequence is assumed, so ( ' ) means "move nothing backwards" and doesn't do anything.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Mouse Moves Change Generator (Generator)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Mouse Moves Change Generator}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you use the mouse to move the Cube and the program has to add a move to the Generator, it will change the last move if they are on the same face and move type.  So if the Generator ends with U and you use the mouse to do another U, the program will pri
nt U\'b2
 in the Generator box.  If you use the mouse to do a U' and the Generator ends with U, the last move will be erased.  (But if you have an equation that needs right parentheses to balance pending + or * operations, they will be added.)  If the program 
inserts or appends a move sequence such as by solving the Cube, the added sequence will be enclosed in parentheses to distinguish it.
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ solving_the_cube}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Solving the Cube}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super 
+{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:009}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Solving the Cube}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 Solving the Cube}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Hints (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Hints
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf1 If you want to solve the Cube yourself but would like a hint, select "Hint" under "He
lp" in the menu.  The hints begin with hint 0, which is no hint at all but explains how the hint feature works.  Hint 1 gives you one very simple clue and hint 12 gives step-by-step directions to solve the Cube.  You should start with hint 0 and work up t
hrough the clues slowly so as not to spoil the puzzle of figuring out the Cube for yourself.
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ All (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  All}{\f1 
\par 
\par Besides solving the Cube yourself, you can let the program do it.  Select "All" under "Solve" in the menu and it will solve the Cube.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ One Step (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  One Step}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you have a scrambled Cube and have edited the main Cube to match (see }{\f1\uldb Editing the Cube}{\v\f1\uldb editing_the_cube}{\f1 
) and you want to follow the program's solution one step at a time, select "One Step" under "Solve" or press the F10 key and the program will make one move toward its solution.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Regenerate (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Regenerate}{\f1 
\par 
\par Several other commands cause the program to unsolve the Cube to produce a Generator to match a scrambled Cube (see }{\f1\uldb The Generator}{\v\f1 the_generator}{\f1 ).  You can "Regenerate" the Generator by selecting this under "Solve".  The progra
m will ask you if you are sure before replacing the Generator with a sequence using its solving algorithm.  The new Generator has the same effect as the old Generator, but if you've made a very long sequence of moves, the machine generated version will be
 a lot smaller.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Edit Cube (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Edit Cube}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you've Edited the Cube, the program automatically produces a new Generator to match your Cube.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Scramble (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Scramble}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can also select the "Scramble" command under "Solve".  The program will ask you if you are sure before moving th
e Cube 500 times at random and producing a new Generator to match.  Of course this command isn't really very useful since getting the Cube scrambled is pretty easy.  This program doesn't use a method that a human being would ordinary recognize for solving
 a Cube, so the Generator you see shouldn't distract you from the job of solving the Cube if you're trying to do it yourself.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Solving Algorithm (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Solving Algorithm}{\f1 
\par 
\par To find its solution, this program uses several data files named COSET*.HHH in the directory with the program
.  These tables and a special algorithm for analyzing the position of the Cube are used to carry out Thistlethwaite's method, described by Morwen Thistlethwaite who proved a Cube could always be solved in 52 moves.  See also }{\f1\uldb Cube History}{
\v\f1 cube_history}{\f1 .
\par 
\par The 
algorithm used by this program is an original implementation of an exhaustive analysis of that method and can solve any Cube in at most 45 moves and about 32 moves on average.  By this method, a move is counted as a quarter or half turn of a single face. 
 This program also carries out an additional one or two whole Cube turns to restore the standard color pattern: Front red, Up blue, Right yellow, Left white, Down green, and Back orange.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Performance (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Performance}{\f1 
\par 
\par Because of the way the tables are compressed, the prog
ram must test the 18 possible face turns to discover which produces the proper step toward a solution.  Since the worse case is 45 moves, the program must analyze less than 810 Cube positions to generate a solution, consuming less time than it requires to
 draw the Cube graphics on the screen.
\par 
\par Because this is a general solution to solving the Cube, it is often possible to make four or five turns on the Cube and confuse the algorithm into solving it the hard way, using 30 moves or so, even though a human bei
ng might easily recognize the four or five original moves and solve it quickly.
\par 
\par Besides strictly following Thistletwaite's method, this program can also search and analyze the tables themselves, producing solutions in fewer moves but requiring more time.  Solution searching is not available in the shareware version.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Search Time (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Search Time}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can select "Search Time" under "Solve" on the menu.  Another sub-menu will appear listing the five commands that invoke the solving algorithm: All, One Step, Regenerate, Scr
amble and Edit Cube.  You can set the search times independently for all five.  Yet another sub-menu is attached to each of these allowing search times from one to 500 seconds.  A check mark appears next to the current selection.  Initially, all the searc
h
 times are "Minimum" and the program stops as soon as it finds any solution.  In this way, the program carries out no search at all.  At the other extreme, you can select "User Cancels" which lets the program run until it finds a known optimum solution, y
ou stop the search while it is running, or somebody unplugs your computer.  The program stores search times in the file HHH.INI.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Progress Window (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Progress Window}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you select a long search time and a command that carries out a search, you can watch your computer's prog
ress in the window that appears.  The window shows the elapsed time, the time limit you specified, the number of moves required by the best solution so far and the time when it was found.  The program also reports the "Back Search Limit."  Initially, this
 
is zero moves, which means that the program only searches through alternatives that adhere to Thistlethwaite's Method.  After searching through these, the program allows the search to digression by one move to see if it has any better luck.  As time goes 
o
n, the search is expanded, but in doing so, the program becomes more and more dependent upon luck.  The program will usually do no better after five hours than it did after five minutes.  It is hard to say exactly what kind of results you'll get, since th
e program isn't just looking up an answer in a table, but searching as fast it can.  But it seems to find a solution in the low twenties of moves in about five minutes.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Concurrent Programs (Solving the Cube)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Concurrent Programs
\par }{\f1 
\par While the search is taking place, this program checks every seco
nd or so to yield to another program waiting to run, so you can run other Windows programs.  But if you do something that isn't time consuming, like typing into another window, Windows will run this program in between keystrokes and so your system will se
em anemic.  You can press the Enter key or click the mouse on "Cancel" to terminate the search.  When the time limit expires or you terminate the search, the best solution is applied to the Generator and the main Cube.
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ cube_files}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Cube Files}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super +{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:010}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cube Files}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 Cube Files}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ New (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  New}{\f1 
\par 
\par You can c
reate a file of Cubes by selecting "New" under "File" on the menu.  The main Cube and its Generator will be copied to the first line of a new file called SCRATCH0.CUB in the same directory as this program.  You can change this name if you want to.  The im
portant thing is that you will get another window listing the contents of a new Cube file.  You can put all the Cubes you want into one file, edit the file and copy to the Clipboard.  You can open several files at once, each with its own window.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ File-Cube Operations (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  File-Cube Operations}{\f1 
\par 
\par The most important thing you can do is double click on an item in a file or press the Enter key.  This will allow you to do an operation between the current item in its file window and the main Cube in its window, see the section }{\f1\uldb File-Cube Op
erations}{\v\f1 file_cube_operations}{\f1 .
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ File Window Anatomy (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  File Window Anatomy}{\f1 
\par 
\par File windows have a different appearance than the main window.  You can only have one main window, but you can have any number of file windows.  A file window contains a list of Cubes.  Each Cube is sho
wn in a small graphics image to the left and its Generator to the right.  If the text is too long for one line to fit across the window, it wraps around for several lines.  If there is still not enough room, the last line ends with three dots ... and you 
c
an't see the rest of it.  You can resize the window to make more room.  A Cube file is stored as ordinary text, one Cube per line, but the first line of the file must be "CUBE FILE".  Only the Generators are stored.  You can only get the Cube graphics wit
h this program, but you can read a Cube file with any text editor.  You can also Cut or Copy to or Paste from the Clipboard.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Menu Commands (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Menu Commands}{\f1 
\par 
\par After you've changed a file you can save it by selecting "Save" under "File" on the menu of the Cube file window. 
 You can also save everything, including the main Cube by selecting "Save All" under "File" on the menu of the main window.  You close a file window by selecting "Close" under "File" on the menu of the file window.  You quit the program completely by sele
cting "Exit" under "File" on the menu of the main window.  When you close a file window or quit the program and some files are out of date, the program will ask you if you want them saved.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ File Names (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  File Names}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you want to change the name of your file, select "
Save As" under "File" in the file window.  You can change the file's name, and directory if you wish.  Once you have saved a file, you will eventually want it back.  In the main window, select "Open" under "File".  If you can remember the file's name, you
 
can open it.  One of the file names you will probably see is SCRATCH.CUB.  Don't open it because it's already open.  That's the file for the Cube in the main window.  If you try to open a file that's already open, you will just land in the open window, wh
i
ch is paneless.  (Sorry.)  Anyway, if you use "Save As" and try to change the name of that file to the name of a file that's already open in another window, the program will stop you, but there is nothing else to prevent you from copying your files over e
ach other.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Current and Selected Items (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Current and Selected Items}{\f1 
\par 
\par When you are in a Cube file window, you can select one or several Cubes for special handling.  There is always one current item in a file window.  This is shown in reverse video, with the background and text colors 
reversed.  If you haven't changed the color scheme on your computer, the background is white and text is black.  The current item has the background black and the text white.  If you drag the mouse across several items or use the Shift and up or down arro
w keys, several items will be highlighted with a blue background, except for the current item which is still black.  (If you've changed your color scheme you'll probably get the color of your active title bar instead of blue.)
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Clipboard (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Clipboard}{\f1 
\par 
\par The items highli
ghted in blue are the ones that you can Cut or Copy to the Clipboard.  If you Cut or Paste, they will disappear.  And if you Paste they will be replaced with the Clipboard.  If no items are highlighted in blue and you Cut or Copy, nothing happens.  If you
 
Paste when no items are highlighted, the Clipboard items will be inserted before the current item in black.  You can also delete the items in blue by pressing the Del key.  If there are no items in blue, it will delete the current item in black.  There is
 also an Undo command.  All these commands can be selected from under "Edit" on the menu.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Last Item (Cube Files)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Last Item}{\f1 
\par 
\par There is also a special item that's at the end of the file, that you can't see unless you move to it and make it the current item.  Then it looks like a 
black dot.  It's there because sometimes you just have to get to the end of the file to move stuff there, or if you delete everything from the file there would be nothing left and there would be no current item, and then where would you be?  If you double
 click on this item or press the Enter key, the program will add the Cube from the main window at the end of the file.
