\cs16\f1\super +{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:011}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ File-Cube Operations}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 
File-Cube Operations}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 When you are in a file window and you double click on an item, or press the Enter key, the program asks yo
u what you want to do.  You have five choices, or you can click on the Cancel button.  Click on your choice and then click on OK.  Cube files are not available in the shareware version.  This is what you can do:
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Insert Main Cube before File Item}{\f1 
\par \tab A copy of the main Cube and its Generator will be inserted into the file above the item you double clicked on.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Copy Main Cube to File Item}{\f1 
\par \tab A copy of the main Cube and its Generator will replace the file item you double clicked on.  The old item is thrown away.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Copy File Item to Main Cube}{\f1 
\par \tab The main Cube and its Generator will be replaced by the file item.  The old main Cube and Generator are thrown away.  The program will put you back in the main window.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Apply File Item Operator to Main Cube}{\f1 
\par \tab The sequence of mov
es in the file item Generator will be inserted into the main Generator and the main Cube will be updated.  The program edits the main Generator so the move sequence from the file is inserted at the spot of the text cursor in the Generator box.  If you had
 marked text in the Generator box, the marked text is deleted and replaced with the file item.  The sequence from the file item is enclosed in parentheses when it is inserted, to distinguish it.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Apply Inverse File Item Operator to Main Cube}{\f1 
\par \tab Same thing onl
y different. The inverse of the sequence of moves in the file item Generator will be inserted into the main Generator and the main Cube will be updated.  The program edits the main Generator so the move sequence from the file is inserted at the spot of th
e
 text cursor in the Generator box.  If you had marked text in the Generator box, the marked text is deleted and replaced with the file item.  The sequence from the file item is enclosed in parentheses and followed by a single quote ' which is called a pri
me or inverse operator.
\par 
\par See also }{\f1\uldb The Generator}{\v\f1 the_generator}{\f1  and }{\f1\uldb Cube Files}{\v\f1 cube_files}{\f1 .
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ cube_history}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Cube History}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super +
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:012}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cube History}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 Cube History}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cube Fad (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Cube Fad}{\f1 
\par 
\par The Rubik's Cube, marketed by Ideal Toy Corp. in the early 1980's, was invented by Erno Rubik in Hungary in 1974.  The patent applicat
ion was filed there on January 30, 1975 and first marketed in 1977.  It was very popular in the West in the early 1980's.  It was a cultural fad which died out after a couple of years.  Relatively few people solved it compared with the number who were fam
iliar with it.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Aesthetics (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Aesthetics}{\f1 
\par 
\par "The cube is certainly one of the best and most elegant puzzles ever made. It is self contained, colorful, a simple shape and it is obvious what the puzzle is."
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 - Puzzles Old & New, How to Make and Solve Them
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
\par The Cube was also unexpected easy to scramble and the mechanism was fascinating because the surfaces moved so many different ways.  These qualities, rather than task of solving it, made it interesting to the vast majority of people.
\par 
\par The Cube's shape and system of labels, 
while elegant, did make the puzzle a good deal more tedious than it needed to be.  It was quickly evident to the user that it was the moving parts, the cubies, that could be rearranged, not the labels themselves; however, it was never possible to view all
 
the labels of more than seven of the 26 cubies at a time, three of these being relatively immobile centers.  The color pattern itself was not intuitive and the labels would probably have benefitted from evidence of the colors that were on adjacent facelet
s.}{\b\v\f1 Solving Algorithms}{\b\f1 
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Solving Algorithms (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Solving Algorithms}{\b\f1 
\par 
\par }{\f1 There were contests of experts who could solve the Cube quickly.  A few could do so in less than one minute.  Solution manuals were published. The mathematician David Singmaster wrote "Notes on Rubik's `Magic Cube
'" in 1980 which discussed the group theoretical aspects of the Cube in detail and included a general solution.  Computers had been used to solve particular problems or to implement these cookbook style solutions, but the first truly computer oriented sol
ution was described by Morwen Thistlethwaite who proved a 52 move solution.
\par 
\par Thistlethwaite's method is based on group theoretical properties and an analysis of the Cube as a whole which is difficult for human beings, but is straightforward when done by com
puter.  The computer can also quickly and accurately index large amounts of data.  Thistlethwaite's method is described in "Handbook of Cubik Math."
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Recent Work (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Recent Work}{\f1 
\par 
\par Eventually, exhaustive analysis proved Thistlethwaite's method could produce a solution in at most 44 moves.
\par 
\par Using the idea of solving the Cube in stages via huge tables, Hans Kloosterman guaranteed a solution in 42 moves.  Herbert Kociemba used this and analysis of suboptimal solutions to claim far fewer moves, but it is probably difficult to 
determine its guaranteed worst case.  Kociemba reports an upper bound of 29 moves.
\par 
\par For more information, visit Mark Longridge's Domain of the Cube on the World Wide Web:
\par http://web.idirect.com/~cubeman
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Metrics (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Metrics}{\f1 
\par 
\par The number of moves described above are q
uarter or half turns of a single face, no slice, middle or antislice moves.  The problem of moving the whole Cube into its standard color pattern is regarded as trivial.  The Cube is solved when the face colors all match.
\par 
\par There is another way of keeping score of computer algorithms which counts half turns as two moves.  Thistlethwaite's method depends heavily on half turns and so this has inspired different approaches.
\par 
\par The minimum worst case number of quarter or half turns required has been proved to be at least 18.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Permutations (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Permutations}{\f1 
\par 
\par There are 12! (factorial) ways to permute the edge cubies.  There are 8! ways to permute the corners.  There are 2^12 ways to flip the edges.  There are 3^8 ways to twist the corners.  If a Cube were dismantled and reassembled 
there are 12! * 8! * 2^12 * 3^8 ways it could be done.  The number of positions a real Cube can reach by twisting the faces is a factor of 12 less because of three factors: a factor of 2 for flip parity, a factor of 3 for twist parity and a factor of 2 fo
r swap parity.  These are discussed a little further in the }{\f1\uldb Editing the Cube}{\v\f1 editing_the_cube}{\f1  section.  The total number of positions is: 43,252,003,274,489,856,000.
\par 
\par }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Physical Dimensions (Cube History)}}}{\b\f1\cf2  Physical Dimensions}{\f1 
\par 
\par A real Cube, marketed by Ideal Toy Corp. in the early 1980's, is about 2 \'bc inches across.  The labels are about 5/8 of an inch across.
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ glossary_and_bibliography}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Glossary and Bibliography}}}{\f1 
 }{\cs16\f1\super +{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super +}{ MAIN:013}}}{\f1  }{\cs16\f1\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Glossary}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 
Glossary and Bibliography}{\f1 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1\cf6 anticlockwise}{\f1  - direction of rotation forward toward left, British term
\par }{\f1\cf6 antiquark}{\f1  - 1/3 counterclockwise twist
\par }{\f1\cf6 antislice move}{\f1  - a move where opposite faces are moved in opposite directions
\par }{\f1\cf6 baryon}{\f1  - 3 quarks or 3 antiquark
\par }{\f1\cf6 center cubie}{\f1  - one of 6 cubies that lie in the center of a face of the cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 chief facelet}{\f1  - on a cubie and cubicle, the reference facelet for twist or flip
\par }{\f1\cf6 clockwise}{\f1  - direction of rotation forward toward right
\par }{\f1\cf6 commutator}{\f1  - X Y X' Y' is the commutator of X and Y sometimes notated [X, Y]
\par }{\f1\cf6 conjugate}{\f1  - X Y X' is the conjugate of Y by X
\par }{\f1\cf6 corner cubie}{\f1  - one of 8 cubies that lies at a corner of the cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 counterclockwise}{\f1  - direction of rotation forward toward left, American term
\par }{\f1\cf6 cube move}{\f1  - a move of the whole cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 cubemeister}{\f1  - person who solves cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 cubicle}{\f1  - an immobile location for a cubie
\par }{\f1\cf6 cubie}{\f1  - one of the 26 minor cubes; 8 corners, 12 edges, 6 centers
\par }{\f1\cf6 cubist}{\f1  - person who uses cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 cubology}{\f1  - the science of the cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 cycle}{\f1  - a continuous set of cubies which interchange cubicles, due to a process
\par }{\f1\cf6 edge cubie}{\f1  - one of 12 cubies that lies in the middle of an edge of the cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 equator}{\f1  - center layer
\par }{\f1\cf6 face}{\f1  - side of cube; surface
\par }{\f1\cf6 facelet}{\f1  - one surface of a cubie
\par }{\f1\cf6 flip}{\f1  - disorientation of an edge cubie
\par }{\f1\cf6 flip parity}{\f1  - the property that total flippancy must be even
\par }{\f1\cf6 flippancy}{\f1  - number of flipped cubies in a cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 fringe}{\f1  - layer less the face
\par }{\f1\cf6 home location}{\f1  - the proper cubicle for a cubie
\par }{\f1\cf6 home position}{\f1  - the proper orientation of a cubie in its home location
\par }{\f1\cf6 inverse move}{\f1  - a move that would undo the original move
\par }{\f1\cf6 Ishige, Terutoski}{\f1  - an inventor of cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 layer}{\f1  - 1/3 of the cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 magic cube}{\f1  - what Rubik calls Rubik's Cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 mathematical problem}{\f1  - how a cube is unscrambled
\par }{\f1\cf6 mechanical problem}{\f1  - how a cube is physically constructed
\par }{\f1\cf6 meson}{\f1  - a quark and an antiquark
\par }{\f1\cf6 middle move}{\f1  - move of the middle layer
\par }{\f1\cf6 monoflip}{\f1  - process causing an edge to flip in place (among other effects)
\par }{\f1\cf6 move}{\f1  - turning one layer (or sometimes more) of the cube; sometimes square moves excluded
\par }{\f1\cf6 orbit}{\f1  - all permutations of cubies producible with processes
\par }{\f1\cf6 plateau state}{\f1  - partially ordered cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 process}{\f1  - sequence of moves; a.k.a. operator, transform, word, tool
\par }{\f1\cf6 quark}{\f1  - 1/3 clockwise twist
\par }{\f1\cf6 quarkscrew}{\f1  - process causing a corner to twist in place (among other effects)
\par }{\f1\cf6 Rubik, Erno}{\f1  - an inventor of cube
\par }{\f1\cf6 sane}{\f1  - proper orientation of a cubie
\par }{\f1\cf6 scrambled cube}{\f1  - not the start position
\par }{\f1\cf6 Singmaster, David}{\f1  - writer of definitive treatise
\par }{\f1\cf6 slice move}{\f1  - move of opposite faces; equivalent to rotating the middle layer
\par }{\f1\cf6 square move}{\f1  - half turn
\par }{\f1\cf6 start position}{\f1  - color of all facelets match on every face, pristine state
\par }{\f1\cf6 subgroup}{\f1  - a subset of possible processes
\par }{\f1\cf6 swap}{\f1  - two cubies exchange cubicles; 2-cycle
\par }{\f1\cf6 swap parity}{\f1  - the property that total number of swaps must be even
\par }{\f1\cf6 Thistlethwaite, Morwen}{\f1  - mathematician who developed breakthrough method
\par }{\f1\cf6 twist}{\f1  - disorientation of a corner cubie; also the sum of twists of all corners
\par }{\f1\cf6 twist parity}{\f1  - the property that total twist must be divisible by three
\par }{\f1\cf6 universe}{\f1  - all permutations of cubies
